          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Does \"with\" require the two terms to sit next to each other in the documentation, or is it an index convention?",
          "answer": "The sources I retrieved answer this, and the answer is that it is an index convention, not a requirement that the terms sit side by side in the record.\n\nAccording to the ICD-10-CM coding summaries, where the Alphabetic Index links two conditions with the word \"with,\" the classification reads that as \"associated with\" or \"due to\" and presumes the relationship. The record does not have to state the link explicitly. The source gives the example of a record listing type 2 diabetes and chronic kidney disease, which is coded as diabetic kidney disease unless the documentation says the two are unrelated. (Source: \"Coding type 2 diabetes: E11 and its complication subcategories,\" and \"ICD-10-CM code structure and the conventions that govern selection.\")\n\nTwo limits apply. First, the presumption holds only for the pairings the index actually lists. Second, it is overridden when the documentation says the two are unrelated. (Source: \"ICD-10-CM code structure and the conventions that govern selection.\")\n\nThe sources do not say whether the two terms must appear close together in the text of a record. They only describe how the index links them. For a precise rule on that, you would need the official ICD-10-CM coding guidelines, which I did not retrieve.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
